---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/sendlane';
const title = 'Sendlane review for Shopify stores';
const desc = 'Evaluate Sendlane account continuity, Privy-group service scope and Shopify retention ownership.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Has Sendlane been acquired?",
    "answer": "Privy announced the acquisition in February 2026 and describes a combined ecommerce email, SMS and onsite direction. Confirm your own account terms and notices."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does that mean every Sendlane account must migrate?",
    "answer": "The cited announcements do not establish a universal closure or migration deadline. Ask the account contact about continuity and any required action."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the benefit of evaluating the group offering?",
    "answer": "Its published positioning combines ecommerce messaging, onsite conversion and human support. Validate the exact product and contracted service scope against your workflows."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is migration support permanent retention management?",
    "answer": "Do not assume that. Specify implementation, training, ongoing optimization, escalation and exclusions in writing, with an internal owner for each task."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long will a transition take and what will it cost?",
    "answer": "Use an account-specific scope, current quote and release gates. Data, sender assets, queues and service work vary; no fixed duration or historical entry price is promised."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should an existing merchant stay or switch?",
    "answer": "Retain a satisfactory account, repair identifiable faults and compare alternatives only against documented unmet needs or total cost. Acquisition alone is not a switching reason."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Sendlane review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <p class="mt-5">Merchant names, workloads and workflows below are proposed planning fixtures, not performed tests or measured client results.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Verdict: account continuity and service scope come first</h2>
      <p>Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane on February 2, 2026. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">announcement</a> emphasizes ecommerce email and SMS, human support and connecting onsite conversion with lifecycle marketing. Those are positive reasons to investigate the group’s offering. They do not establish every existing account’s roadmap, price or service entitlement.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> presents a unified email, SMS and onsite direction. It does not specify a universal shutdown date or mandatory migration for all Sendlane customers. Keep a functioning account when the agreed service and workflows meet requirements; obtain account-specific instructions before making changes. New prospects should confirm which product and contract they are actually buying.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">A continuity worksheet for existing accounts and new buyers</h2>
      <p>Existing merchant: confirm login, connected Shopify shop, billing entity, renewal date, sender domains, SMS numbers and pending queues. Ask the account contact which features and support channels continue, what changes require action, and whether there is any account-specific transition notice. Archive written answers with a named merchant owner rather than extrapolating from acquisition marketing.</p>
      <p>New buyer: request a demonstration of the exact proposed environment, including the form-to-profile handoff, cart recovery and reporting export. Confirm implementation tasks, technical escalation, response targets, training and excluded services in writing. Human assistance can reduce setup work, but it is not proof of a permanent managed retention program or a guaranteed recovery result.</p>
      <p>For any proposed transition within the group, inventory historical reports, consent evidence, templates, suppression lists, segment definitions, integrations and data-export access. Identify whether IDs, domains or phone assets change and who handles them. Staying, repairing an integration or accepting a documented transition are all valid outcomes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Supplement cart repair before considering a switch</h2>
      <p>Proposed supplement merchant: $140k monthly sales and 22,000 profiles with duplicate reminders to recent purchasers. This is a planning fixture, not a migration we performed. Trace the current order and checkout IDs, replay behavior, purchase exclusions and pending messages. Repair a broken exclusion in the existing account if that solves the problem; a duplicate email alone does not justify changing vendors.</p>
      <p>If the merchant evaluates a new environment, ask the implementation team to rebuild the same three-touch recovery logic and explain it to the internal owner. Verify every trigger, field and exit in that account. Use stable cart and message identifiers, current purchase checks and channel-specific permission. Test order placement during a delay, an opt-out before SMS and a duplicate event before enabling a cohort.</p>
      <p>Keep one active recovery sender per cart. Stop incumbent enrollment, inspect queued sends and cancel or drain them before enabling the replacement. A proposed comparison uses disjoint cohorts or shadow evaluation, not duplicate live reminders. Rehearse rollback with staff accounts and document which sender resumes ownership.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Subscription and one-time purchases: ownership of the branch</h2>
      <p>Proposed supplement order contains a subscription and a one-time item. Map line-item subscription status and current subscription state through the actual Shopify or subscription integration; do not infer it from a product name. Show education on the subscription path and consider a subscription offer only for an eligible one-time buyer.</p>
      <p>Test mixed baskets, subscription cancellation, paused renewal, refund and missing subscription data. The fallback should omit a misleading upsell and send only appropriate permitted content. A calendar delay is not proof that the customer is still subscribed. Ask whether support builds this branch, documents it or merely advises on it, and assign a merchant owner for subsequent changes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Fashion browse and home-goods winback fixtures</h2>
      <p>Proposed fashion store at 720 orders monthly: pass denim interest from a Justuno quiz or other capture form with a timestamp and source. Demonstrate that the exact proposed product can use the mapped value for the intended audience and content. Test missing identity, multiple collection memberships, a changed tag and a purchase before dispatch. Avoid treating historic Sendlane descriptions as guaranteed parity with every new Privy-group account.</p>
      <p>Proposed home-goods customer last purchased 95 days ago. Define a 90-day eligibility rule and consider an SMS follow-up only if separately permitted and within the merchant’s frequency budget. Re-evaluate purchase, opt-out and refund status before each touch. Open tracking alone should not determine whether a customer needs another message.</p>
      <p>For a 380-order shop, compare retaining its existing tool with the quoted software and service package. There is no order-volume threshold at which a premium automatically pays for itself. Test an actual missing requirement, including a capture-to-welcome handoff, before adding cost or migration work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Glow Theory: insourcing without losing institutional knowledge</h2>
      <p>Glow Theory is a hypothetical beauty brand at $210k monthly sales and 3,400 orders. It is considering replacing some agency work with an internal marketing lead. The service question is which tasks the vendor team will perform and which move to that lead: creative, segmentation, testing, integration repairs, reporting and deliverability need separate owners.</p>
      <p>Archive current flow definitions and results before changing anything. Translate VIP rules and product-use preferences into explicit data requirements. Predictive scores and agency knowledge do not automatically transfer as portable logic; confirm exportable fields and destination equivalents. Preserve gift-buyer exclusions and separate the gift recipient’s permission from the purchaser’s.</p>
      <p>Keep agency help for unresolved work if necessary. A completed onboarding call does not prove the internal lead can edit a replenishment branch safely, resolve a refund edge case or operate during peak traffic. Ask the lead to demonstrate recent-purchaser suppression and VIP access, then record gaps and training needs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Release gates, escalation and deliverability</h2>
      <p>First gate: confirm written account continuity or transition scope, data access, quote and responsible contacts. Second gate: authenticate domains, inspect sender assets and map eligible contacts with current opt-outs. Third gate: rehearse welcome, cart, post-purchase and winback independently, including queue cancellation and rollback. Expand traffic only when the merchant’s evidence supports it; no fixed migration duration is promised.</p>
      <p>Choose change windows around the shop’s actual promotion calendar and operational capacity. Ask for a deliverability plan appropriate to its sender history rather than assuming every account needs the same IP treatment. A giveaway import needs permission and engagement review before activation. No inbox-placement recovery or three-week improvement is asserted here.</p>
      <p>Agree escalation contacts and response expectations before relying on support during a sale. Store the segment glossary, field mapping and change log internally. Support can help troubleshoot within the contracted scope; the merchant still needs an owner who can stop a mistaken promotion and explain why a customer entered a flow.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Current quotes and hypothetical agency economics</h2>
      <p>Use identical workload inputs across candidates: 14,000 contacts, 38,000 monthly emails and 2,000 SMS for one proposed fashion case; 22,000 profiles for the supplement case. Include billable definitions, peak volumes, destinations, SMS segments, service scope, implementation work and renewal terms. Historical entry prices and assumed discounts are not current quotes.</p>
      <p>Illustrative Glow Theory assumptions: prior agency $4,200 plus software $680 equals $4,880 monthly. Assume a replacement software-and-service quote of $450–750, not a verified price. Cash difference is $4,130–4,430. Fifteen internal hours at $45 cost $675, leaving $3,455–3,755 before implementation, message overages and any performance change. Removing agency work only creates savings if its tasks are covered.</p>
      <p>Illustrative risk: 2,368 eligible carts, a recovery decline from 12% to 8%, and $95 order value imply 94.72 fewer expected orders and $8,998.40 revenue difference. Round only after calculation. Use the same denominator and window; this is sensitivity arithmetic, not proof of loss caused by a platform. Apply margin and returns before comparing with saved fees. A separate $200 SMS overrun is a budget assumption.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Weekly operation and a retention decision</h2>
      <p>Monday: compare flow eligibility, complaints, deduplicated orders and contribution by channel. Wednesday: inspect consent, SMS spend and product mappings. Friday: review pending queues, unresolved support tickets and upcoming promotions. Monthly: review internal hours and remaining agency tasks alongside the software invoice; a lower subscription is insufficient if labor or missed work rises.</p>
      <p>Retain the existing account if service continuity, workflow behavior and economics remain satisfactory. Repair configuration when the evidence points to an identifiable mapping or suppression fault. Consider a documented group transition or another supplier only when requirements, support or total cost justify the change. Confirm the actual destination and rollback path before authorizing cutover.</p>
      <p>Compare the same fixtures with <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a>, <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> and <a href="/apps/rejoiner">Rejoiner</a>. The <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend comparison</a>, <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart guide</a> and <a href="/compare">comparison hub</a> provide additional questions, not an unsupported vendor ranking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sendlane Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
